// Practice quiz: == vs equals
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/classes-and-objects/double-equals-vs-equals

::o1 primitives::int a \= 1000; int b \= 1000; What is a \=\= b? {
=true#Correct. For primitives, \=\= compares the values themselves.
~false, because they are two different variables#Not quite. Two primitive variables with the same value compare equal.
~A compile error#Not quite. \=\= between two ints is allowed.
~It depends on the JVM#Not quite. Comparing primitive values with \=\= is always well defined.
}

::o1 builder equals::StringBuilder x \= new StringBuilder("ab"); StringBuilder y \= new StringBuilder("ab"); What is x.equals(y)? {
~true, because both hold "ab"#Not quite. Content comparison needs an equals override, which StringBuilder lacks.
~A compile error#Not quite. equals is defined on every object, so the call compiles.
~true, but only after toString()#Not quite. x.toString().equals(y.toString()) is true, but x.equals(y) is still false.
=false#Correct. StringBuilder does not override equals, so it inherits the Object version, which compares references.
}

::o2 content test::String input holds text the user typed. Which condition is true when input holds yes? {
~input \=\= "yes"#Not quite. \=\= compares references, and typed text is a different String object from the literal.
=input.equals("yes")#Correct. equals compares the characters of the two Strings.
~input \= "yes"#Not quite. That is an assignment; its String result is not a boolean, so it does not compile as a condition.
~input.compareTo("yes")#Not quite. compareTo returns an int, which is not a condition by itself.
}

::o2 run-time text::Why can input \=\= "quit" be false even when the user typed quit? {
~\=\= ignores the last character#Not quite. \=\= does not look at characters at all.
~Typed text always ends with a space#Not quite. Reading methods such as nextLine do not add a trailing space.
~\=\= can only compare numbers#Not quite. \=\= compiles for references too; it just compares the references.
=Text read while the program runs is a new String object, and \=\= compares references#Correct. The characters match but the objects differ, so only equals reports a match.
}

::o3 inherited equals::class Point declares fields but does not override equals. What does p.equals(q) compare? {
~Whether every field matches#Not quite. Field-by-field comparison happens only if the class overrides equals.
~The text that toString returns#Not quite. equals does not call toString.
=Whether p and q refer to the same object#Correct. The equals inherited from Object compares identity, just like \=\=.
~Nothing; the call does not compile#Not quite. Every class inherits equals from Object.
}

::o3 override signature::Which declaration in class Point overrides equals from Object? {
~public boolean equals(Point o)#Not quite. A Point parameter makes a new overload; calls with an Object argument still use the inherited version.
=public boolean equals(Object o)#Correct. It matches the inherited signature exactly, so it overrides it.
~boolean equals(Object o)#Not quite. Without public it narrows the inherited access, which the compiler rejects.
~public int equals(Object o)#Not quite. The return type must stay boolean.
}
